@prefix intention: <http://example.org/agora/intention#> .
@prefix desire: <http://example.org/agora/desire#> .
@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix ssn:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/agora/intention> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Agora intention" ;
    rdfs:comment """BDI's third letter: a commitment to reduce a named gap by a named means, which persists until it is resolved rather than being re-decided on every perception.

**An intention is an amortised deliberation.** The sharpest lesson in the BDI literature is that intentions exist to SAVE deliberation — an agent that re-decides on every reading is a reflex machine wearing a planner's name — and here that is a cost model rather than philosophy: deliberation is about to include an LLM call, and the commitment is what makes one affordable. This package is the substrate that makes that persistence a FACT with a lifecycle instead of a scatter of module state: `bidding.pending`, a held voucher and a commanded cadence were all intentions before this package named them.

**Nothing here decides.** Deciding what to commit to is deliberation's — the welded reflex chain today, a family with an LLM member later. This package only KEEPS: it records an adoption, resolves it as satisfied or dropped with the reason, refuses to re-adopt what is already standing, and answers what stands. The seam between keeping and deciding is the whole reason they are two capabilities.

See knowledge/decisions/an-intention-is-an-amortised-deliberation.md.""" .

#################  The capability  #################

intention:IntentionCapability a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "Intention capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "The family. Anything that keeps this agent's commitments is one of these, so a sibling may say 'commit to this' and ask what stands without knowing how commitments are kept." .

intention:Keeping a intention:IntentionCapability ; rdfs:label "Keeping" ;
    rdfs:comment "Keeps commitments by rule: a standing intention blocks re-adoption of the same one until it resolves or outlives the agent's patience. Granted by a STAKE AND A LEVER — acting for a subject that states needs, plus at least one way to act on them — because wanting without means and means without wants both leave nothing to commit to." .

#################  Where intentions are kept  #################
#
#  A graph of the agent's own, like its beliefs and its revisions, and NOT public: an intention
#  disclosed is strategy leaked. The BID is the public face of an intention to acquire — the
#  market sees what you do, never what you are trying to bring about. The graph is named from
#  the one identifier a process is given; see graphs.py.

#################  What an intention IS  #################

intention:Intention a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Intention" ;
    rdfs:comment """One commitment: reduce the gap in one property, by one means, adopted at a moment, resolved at another or still standing. `ssn:forProperty` names the property — the same term a region, a condition and an aim already use, so the gap an intention is about is joinable to the desire it serves by the vocabulary everything else speaks.

The lifecycle is two timestamps and an outcome, not a state machine: standing is `adoptedAt` without `resolvedAt`, and how it ended is `outcome` — the same shape review:Revision has, for the same reason. A dropped intention keeps its `becauseOf`, because a commitment abandoned without a reason is indistinguishable from one forgotten.""" .

intention:by a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain intention:Intention ; rdfs:range intention:Means ;
    rdfs:label "by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The kind of act committed to. A MEANS, not a plan: the how stays with whichever capability executes it." .

intention:Means a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Means" .

intention:Observe a intention:Means ; rdfs:label "Observe" ;
    rdfs:comment "Look: obtain a reading where the gap is unmeasured or too stale to act on. The first intention every agent adopts, because at birth there is a desired state and no observations — see the decision record. Satisfied by the reading arriving; dropped when whatever needed it stops waiting." .

intention:Acquire a intention:Means ; rdfs:label "Acquire" ;
    rdfs:comment "Obtain what would reduce a gap — here, bid in a market. Adopted when a bid is submitted, satisfied by the voucher, and standing in between, which is exactly the state `bidding` used to keep as an untyped dict." .

intention:Apply a intention:Means ; rdfs:label "Apply" ;
    rdfs:comment "Spend a held claim against the world. RESERVED: declared because the vocabulary should be honest that winning and applying are different commitments, and unimplemented because in the spot market nothing stands between them — a voucher is redeemed the moment it is issued, so there is no held claim for a commitment to be about. The futures market is where this becomes real: win a voucher redeemable until exp, and HOLDING it is this intention. See the roadmap's futures seam." .

intention:adoptedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain intention:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "adopted at" .
intention:resolvedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain intention:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "resolved at" ;
    rdfs:comment "Absent while the intention stands. Its absence IS the standing state — no flag to keep in step." .
intention:outcome a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain intention:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "outcome" ;
    rdfs:comment "How it ended: satisfied or dropped. A string rather than a class per outcome, exactly as review:outcome is." .
intention:becauseOf a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain intention:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "because of" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why it was adopted, and — appended on resolution — why it ended. Prose for the operator reading a ledger, never parsed." .

#################  The commitment policy — a belief, bounded here  #################
#
#  How stubborn to be is an OPINION, so it is each agent's belief and review may move it. The
#  BOUNDS are constitutional and live on the family, exactly as perception's cadence bounds do:
#  an agent that re-decides every second has retired the whole point of committing, and one that
#  never reconsiders will honour a dead commitment forever.

intention:patienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "patience seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long a standing intention blocks re-adoption of the same one. Within it, a second impulse to do the same thing is ABSORBED — that is the amortisation, and with an LLM deliberator it is the cost model. Past it, a new adoption supersedes the old, which is recorded as dropped with the reason." .

intention:minPatienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "minimum patience" .
intention:maxPatienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "maximum patience" .

intention:IntentionCapability intention:minPatienceS 10 ; intention:maxPatienceS 86400 .
